Dear 3D Printing Industry Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released after 220 curated, user-visible changes
since the first public 0.1 demo. It is desktop software for adapting imported
3D models, constructing printable parts from dimensions and tested components,
and preparing them for an external slicer. The news is not another generative
AI front end; it is a local, reproducible model-preparation workflow that uses
AI only as an optional control layer.

The new release can recognise a hole in an imported STL or 3MF model and
change its diameter while preserving the original position and axis. It adds
in-viewport constrained sketching, reusable parametric parts assembled from
operation history without executable code, standards-based fasteners and
bearing seats, and material/process settings for each physical filament spool.
Those per-spool values are carried into the correct 3MF material slot and on
to an external slicer.

Every geometry change remains a parameterised operation. The optional LLM can
invoke the same registered API as menus, but cannot calculate geometry or run
generated source. Without AI, an account or an internet connection, all model
editing, analysis and export remain available. Solidon runs on Windows, macOS
and Linux.

The complete public demo is free until 30 October 2026. Version 1.0 is planned
for 1 November as a €69 one-time licence including 1.x updates, moving later
to €99. Activation is designed to work once and remain offline, including a
file-based route for air-gapped workstations.

Product: https://solidon3d.de/en/
